  "text": "Here's the completed function `remove_adjacent_duplicates` that removes consecutive duplicates, and the test cases work as expected:\n\n```python\nimport unittest\n\ndef remove_adjacent_duplicates(lst):\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n    \n    result = [lst[0]]\n    for num in lst[1:]:\n        if num != result[-1]:\n            result.append(num)\n    return result\n\nclass TestRemoveAdjacentDuplicates(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_basic_functionality(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]), [1, 2, 3, 4])\n    \n    def test_repeated_elements(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([5, 5, 5, 6, 7, 7]), [5, 6, 7])\n    \n    def test_multiple_duplicates(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5]), [1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    unittest.main()\n```\n\n### How it works:\n- The function takes a list of integers.\n- It initializes a result list with the first element.\n- It then iterates through the list, and only appends elements to the result if they are different from the last element in the result.\n- This ensures that **only consecutive duplicates are removed**, and the result list only contains non-consecutive (adjacent) elements.\n\n### Example:\n```python\nremove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4])  # returns [1, 2, 3, 4]\n```\n\nThis implementation is efficient with **O(n)** time complexity and **O(n)** space complexity.",
